Congenital Torticollis
Paediatric & Neonatal
Head tilted toward affected side, Chin rotated away, Palpable sternocleidomastoid mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-014
Reason for Visit: Acute caregiver alarm upon discovering a hard neck mass and the infant's inability to turn their head, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out acute cervical spine injury or malignant lymphadenopathy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a 3-week-old infant with a sudden caregiver observation of a firm neck lump and severe restriction of cervical movement. Immediate assessment was required to definitively exclude an acute parapharyngeal abscess, cervical spine abnormality, or malignant mass prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a hard lump on the side of their neck, and they are completely stuck looking over their left shoulder."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a persistent, visibly abnormal head tilt directly leaning toward the right side. The chin is continually rotated, pointing explicitly away from the affected side (toward the left). The caregivers just noticed a unilateral, firm, palpable, olive-like mass strictly located on the right side of the neck. There is severely restricted active and passive cervical range of motion when the caregiver attempts to feed the infant on the contralateral side. The infant is 3 weeks old. The birth history involves a difficult, assisted vaginal delivery using forceps.
Functional Status: Feeding well but only comfortable in one position.
Social/Safety Concerns: High parental anxiety regarding the "tumor-like" mass and fear of permanently breaking the infant's neck during handling.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Not measured | HR: 140 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 30 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 4.2 Kg
General: Alert, vigorous infant, resting comfortably with the head tilted to the right and chin rotated to the left.
Physical Exam: Palpation of the right anterior neck reveals a distinct, unilateral, firm, non-tender, olive-like mass strictly located within the body of the right sternocleidomastoid muscle. There is a palpable fibrotic band along the muscle. Cervical lymph nodes are not enlarged. Passive cervical range of motion is physically restricted by muscular tightness, not bony block. The clavicles are intact bilaterally with no crepitus, ruling out clavicular fracture. No facial asymmetry or plagiocephaly is yet evident.
Environmental Observations: Cot positioned such that the infant must look right to see the door and caregivers.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Congenital Torticollis - Status: Stable/Newly identified
Differentials:

Cervical Lymphadenitis / Abscess - Ruled out due to the absence of fever, overlying skin erythema, fluctuance, or severe tenderness to palpation over the mass.

Clavicular Fracture - Ruled out due to intact clavicles without crepitus, normal symmetrical spontaneous movement of both arms, and a normal Moro reflex.

Klippel-Feil Syndrome (Cervical Spine Anomaly) - Ruled out clinically by the presence of the distinct muscular mass (fibromatosis colli) explaining the mechanical restriction, though formal imaging is required for absolute exclusion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The discovery of a firm neck mass and severely restricted neck movement in a neonate mandated an urgent after-hours assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified the high parental distress and the critical need to exclude acute infectious processes (abscess) or occult trauma (clavicular fracture). Objective clinical evaluation confirmed the classic diagnostic tetrad of congenital torticollis secondary to fibromatosis colli, successfully ruling out acute surgical or infectious emergencies. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in profound, unnecessary parental terror regarding a presumed malignancy and delayed the initiation of crucial early physical therapy. Immediate intervention provided diagnostic clarity and safely guided initial conservative management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign nature of the muscular mass. Referral generated for pediatric physiotherapy.
Positive Behavioral Change: During awake, supervised tummy time, always approach the infant and offer high-contrast toys from their non-preferred side. Rewarding the infant's effort to turn their head with social engagement and visual stimulation directly utilizes positive reinforcement to gently and actively stretch the shortened sternocleidomastoid muscle without forceful passive stretching.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Pediatric physiotherapy referral.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1-2 weeks to arrange standard cervical ultrasonography to formalize the diagnosis and monitor therapy progress.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the mass rapidly increases in size, becomes red and hot, if the infant develops a fever, or stops moving their arms or legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; ultrasound is best practice to definitively exclude structural anomalies.
Clinical Course: With dedicated, daily physical therapy, the fibrotic mass typically resolves and full range of motion is restored by 6 to 12 months of age.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the lump turns red, feels hot, or if the baby develops a high temperature.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lump is a benign knot of tight muscle tissue caused by positioning in the womb or a difficult birth. It is not a tumor or an infection. The muscle needs to be gently stretched over time.
Actionable Steps: Alternate the direction the baby sleeps in the cot so they have to turn their head the difficult way to see you when they wake up. Do not aggressively force the neck to turn.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Congenital Torticollis (tight neck muscle). Not a tumor or infection. Start gentle positioning techniques. GP review in 1-2 weeks.

SMS EDUCATION

Encourage baby to look towards the difficult side using toys and your voice. Go to ED if the lump becomes red, hot, or baby gets a fever.
